THE COUNT HARNESS READS ITS RULE LIST FROM /tmp AND FAILS BY UNDERCOUNTING

genLadder/countPop.sh ends with

    done < /tmp/b2/rules.txt

and its per-row generator is /tmp/b2/mk.py. Neither file is in the repository.
Both are scratch from the F-31 campaign of 2026-08-23.

WHY THIS IS THE LYING-INSTRUMENT CLASS AND NOT AN INCONVENIENCE

A harness that cannot find its INPUT does not error. It reads an empty or short
list, runs the rows it was given, and prints a smaller headline with the same
confident wording:

    THE COUNT: N compiled clean, M parse-failed, K crashed, of N+M+K attempted

There is no line in that output that says how many rules there ARE, so a run
over three rules and a run over forty-two are typographically identical. The
number simply gets smaller, and a smaller number of failures reads as PROGRESS.

This is the shape CLAUDE.md's harness rules exist for. H1 says a harness echoes
the binary it is testing, for exactly this reason -- so a stale instrument shows
up as a diff in the log rather than as a mystery. The rule list is the harness's
OTHER input and gets no such treatment. H9's corollary applies too: a count is a
value, and a wrong one arrives wearing the shape of a right one.

⚠ IT HAS ALREADY HAPPENED ONCE. On 2026-08-24 rules.txt was GONE -- /tmp/b2
survived with eight other files from that session and rules.txt was not among
them. The list had to be reconstructed by scraping rule names out of the PRIOR
RUN'S OUTPUT (/tmp/b2/count.out), which is a circular source: it can only ever
return the rules the last run happened to attempt, so a list that had already
silently shrunk would have been faithfully reproduced as correct. The
reconstruction was checked against the true population -- the incantation above
prints it -- and the re-run reproduced 22/19/1 byte-identical, so nothing was
lost that day. That check was a choice, not a property of the harness.

⚠⚠ AND THE RUN ABOVE IS NO LONGER HYPOTHETICAL. The incantation applies THE
HARNESS'S OWN FOUR FILTERS -- not noPrint, not a bin, is a rule, actionType
zero, exactly incant/f31's fbWalk -- and counts 58. The list the harness read
held 42. Sixteen rules qualify and were never attempted.

WHAT THAT DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH, because the difference matters. It does
NOT establish that the original rules.txt was wrong: the 42-row list used on
2026-08-24 was RECONSTRUCTED from the prior run's output, so it can only contain
rules that run had attempted, and if the list had already shrunk the
reconstruction would faithfully reproduce the shrunken version as if correct.
That is the circularity named above, now with a number attached to it. What it
DOES establish is that nothing in the harness can tell 42 from 58, which is the
entire complaint.

